CH = dict(num=6, title="How Forces Affect Motion", tag="Physics", theme="Force, friction, Newton’s three laws, systems of objects",
intro="Chapter 5 described motion; this chapter asks **what causes a change in motion**. It covers the nature and measurement of **force**, **balanced and unbalanced forces**, the role of **friction**, and **Newton’s three laws of motion** with their everyday applications — from catching a cricket ball and airbags to walking, rowing a canoe and launching a rocket.",
big_questions=["Why does a canoe move forward when the canoeist pushes water backwards, and faster when pushing harder?", "Will an empty canoe or one carrying a passenger move faster for the same paddle force?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "6.1 The Concept of Force"),
("ul", ["A **force** can make an object move from rest, change its speed or direction, or change its shape (kicking a ball, a bat striking a ball, squeezing a lemon).",
 "Force is a **vector**: it has magnitude and direction. If either changes, its effect changes. Examples with direction: friction opposes motion; magnets attract/repel along a line; the Earth pulls objects downward (gravitational force); a liquid pushes an object upward (buoyant force).",
 "**SI unit: newton (N)** — written with a small ‘n’ for the unit name (newton), capital N for the symbol.",
 "Magnitude can be measured with a **spring balance** (weight = gravitational force by the Earth). Smallest force we feel is about a millinewton (10<sup>−3</sup> N); specialised experiments measure down to yoctonewtons (10<sup>−24</sup> N)."]),

("h2", "6.2 Balanced and Unbalanced Forces"),
("ul", ["Usually several forces act together: pushing a box (applied force + friction opposite to motion); a ball floating on water (gravitational force down, buoyant force up).",
 "**Tug of war:** equal and opposite pulls → **balanced forces**, rope does not move; unequal pulls → **unbalanced forces**, rope moves along the larger force."]),
("table", ["Situation", "Net force", "Direction"], [
 ["Two forces in the **same** direction", "Sum of magnitudes", "Same direction"],
 ["Two forces in **opposite** directions", "Difference of magnitudes", "Along the larger force"],
 ["Equal and opposite", "Zero (balanced)", "None"]]),
("table", ["Example (10 N and 6 N)", "Net force"], [["Both to the right", "16 N to the right"], ["10 N right, 6 N left", "4 N to the right"], ["6 N right, 10 N left", "4 N to the left"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "An object’s motion depends only on the **net force**. A weightlifter holding a barbell steady: weight (down) and her upward push are balanced. Barbell of 30 kg: F = mg = 30 × 9.8 = **294 N** upward. In arm-wrestling, when the arm tilts, the forces are unbalanced and the player who exerts more force wins."),
("ul", ["Forces at an angle, or equal and opposite forces on the two ends of an extended object (which make it rotate, like a tap or handlebar), are studied in higher classes."]),

("h2", "6.3 Friction — Often Overlooked, Always Present"),
("ul", ["Friction acts between surfaces in contact, **opposite to the direction of motion (or attempted motion)**. A box does not move until the applied force exceeds friction so that a net force acts forward.",
 "Other forces on a box: **weight** (gravitational, downward) and the **normal force** (exerted by the surface, upward, perpendicular to it) — these balance each other. Air also exerts a friction (drag) that is often small enough to ignore.",
 "A bicycle or ball stops after you stop pushing because friction acts against motion. A force is needed to *counter friction* to keep a body moving; no force is needed to keep it moving at constant velocity if friction is absent."]),
("h3", "Activity 6.1 and 6.2 — friction depends on the surfaces"),
("steps", [("Step 1", "Stack four ₹10 coins with tape. Mark points A, B and C on a surface; stretch a rubber band back to C using the stack and release."),
 ("Step 2", "Repeat on wood, laminated top and polished marble/tile with the same stretch. Measure the distance travelled each time."),
 ("Step 3", "Result: the smoother the surface, the **farther** the stack travels and the **more slowly** its velocity decreases — friction is smaller."),
 ("Step 4", "With a spring balance pulling a wooden block, the reading when the block just moves (at constant velocity) approximates the friction. Smallest reading on the surface where the stack went farthest; largest where it went least.")]),
("ul", ["Before release the stack is stationary, so forces are balanced. After release the rubber band’s force exceeds friction → net force forward → acceleration. Once contact with the band is lost, only friction acts (backward) and the stack slows and stops."]),
("h3", "Thought experiment (Galileo)"),
("ul", ["With **zero friction** on a horizontal surface, the object would never slow down and would keep moving forever. For ages it was wrongly believed that a force is needed to maintain motion; in the 17th century **Galileo** argued through thought experiments that if all impediments are removed, a body continues to move indefinitely."]),

("h2", "6.4 Newton’s First Law of Motion"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**First law (law of inertia):** An object at rest remains at rest, and an object in motion continues to move with a constant velocity, unless a net force acts on it."),
("ul", ["**Inertia** (Newton’s word) = the tendency of objects to resist a change in their state of rest or uniform motion.",
 "If net force = 0, then acceleration = 0. ‘Constant velocity’ means no change in magnitude **or** direction (a straight line at uniform speed).",
 "Example: a person pushes a moving box with force equal to friction → net force zero → box continues at constant velocity.",
 "Graphs for zero net force: **at rest** — position–time horizontal line, velocity–time line along the time axis; **constant velocity** — position–time straight sloping line, velocity–time horizontal line above the axis.",
 "A real object seldom has *no* force on it; the law is achieved when forces cancel (e.g. weight balanced by normal force, applied force balanced by friction).",
 "Questions: an object moving with constant velocity has **zero net force**; with no net force it can stay at rest or move uniformly, but cannot have constant acceleration."]),

("h2", "6.5 Newton’s Second Law of Motion"),
("ul", ["A force produces acceleration. Hypotheses tested: (1) for the same object, larger force → larger acceleration; (2) for the same force, larger mass → smaller acceleration.",
 "**Activities 6.3 and 6.4 (cart–pulley demonstration):** a falling cup of known mass pulls a cart with constant force through a thread over a pulley. Starting from rest, s = ½ a T². With the same distance, a<sub>1</sub>T<sub>1</sub>² = a<sub>2</sub>T<sub>2</sub>², so shorter time means larger acceleration. Doubling the pulling weight raises acceleration (a little less than double because of wheel friction); doubling the cart mass lowers acceleration."]),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Second law:** When a net force acts on an object, it accelerates in the direction of the net force; the acceleration is **directly proportional to the net force** and **inversely proportional to the mass**."),
("note", "FORMULA", "a = F ÷ m   or   **F = ma**   (F in newton, m in kg, a in m s<sup>−2</sup>).   **1 N = 1 kg m s<sup>−2</sup>** — the force that gives 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m s<sup>−2</sup>.   Weight = **F = mg**, g = 9.8 m s<sup>−2</sup> (≈ 10 for quick estimates), independent of the mass of the object."),
("ul", ["A 100 g mass resting on your palm presses with about **1 N**.",
 "More general form: force = rate of change of **momentum** (momentum = mass × velocity), valid even when mass changes."]),
("h3", "Applications — stretching the time reduces the force"),
("ul", ["**Cricket catch:** the fielder draws the hands backward while catching, increasing the time to stop the ball, so acceleration and force are smaller and injury is avoided.",
 "**Airbags (with seat belts):** inflate into a soft cushion; the passenger’s head and chest take longer to stop, so the force is reduced.",
 "**High-jump landing mat/sand bed, bubble wrap or hay for glassware:** lengthen the stopping time and cut the force.",
 "**Cracking a coconut:** it stops in a very short time on a hard surface, so the ground exerts a very large force and the shell breaks."]),
("table", ["Problem", "Working", "Answer"], [
 ["Block 25 kg, max friction 50 N; push 50 N for 2 s", "Net force 0 → balanced", "Remains at rest"],
 ["Same block, push 55 N for 2 s", "F = 5 N; a = 5/25 = 0.2; s = ½ × 0.2 × 4", "a = 0.2 m s<sup>−2</sup>; s = 0.4 m"],
 ["Sports car 1500 kg (v–t graph): 0 → 10 m s<sup>−1</sup> in 5 s east", "a = 2; F = 1500 × 2", "3000 N east"],
 ["Same car at constant 10 m s<sup>−1</sup> for 5 s", "a = 0", "0 N"],
 ["Same car slows from 10 m s<sup>−1</sup> to 0 in 5 s", "a = −2; F = 1500 × (−2)", "−3000 N (towards west)"],
 ["Toy car 100 g moving at constant 0.5 m s<sup>−1</sup>", "Constant velocity", "Net force = 0"],
 ["Bullet 50 g at 100 m s<sup>−1</sup> stops in 50 cm", "0 = 100² + 2a(0.5) → a = −10<sup>4</sup> m s<sup>−2</sup>; F = 0.05 × 10<sup>4</sup>", "500 N"],
 ["Football 0.4 kg kicked to 108 km h<sup>−1</sup> (30 m s<sup>−1</sup>) by 800 N", "a = 800/0.4 = 2000; t = 30/2000", "0.015 s"],
 ["2 kg object at 10 m s<sup>−1</sup>, friction 7 N + 3 N opposing", "Net 10 N; a = −5; s = 100/(2 × 5)", "10 m"],
 ["Harrow m<sub>1</sub> gives a<sub>1</sub>; trolley m<sub>2</sub> gives a<sub>2</sub> under the same F", "m<sub>1</sub> = F/a<sub>1</sub>, m<sub>2</sub> = F/a<sub>2</sub>; a = F/(m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>)", "a = a<sub>1</sub>a<sub>2</sub> ÷ (a<sub>1</sub> + a<sub>2</sub>)"],
 ["Snake boat: 100 oarsmen × 200 N, 5 rowing wrongly", "(95 − 5) × 200", "18,000 N forward"]]),
("note", "REMEMBER", "For two children on identical swings, the heavier child needs a larger force for the same initial acceleration (F = ma). Tractor/trolley and similar questions: always add the masses when objects move together."),

("h2", "6.6 Newton’s Third Law of Motion"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Third law:** Whenever one object exerts a force on a second object, the second object simultaneously exerts an **equal and opposite force** on the first. (Action and reaction.)"),
("ul", ["Forces always occur in **pairs** and act on **two different objects**, so they **do not cancel**. (Two equal and opposite forces on the *same* object do balance.)",
 "Activity 6.5: sitting on a wheeled chair and pushing a heavy table forward, you move backward; pulling the table, you move toward it.",
 "Activity 6.6: two spring balances hooked together and pulled show **identical readings** — the forces are equal in magnitude.",
 "The law holds for **all** forces, contact or non-contact: two magnets, two charged balloons, the Earth and a fruit."]),
("table", ["Example", "Action–reaction pair"], [
 ["Walking/running", "Foot pushes ground backward; ground (through **friction**) pushes you forward. Without friction the foot would slip. Grooves on soles and treads on tyres increase friction; wet floors, ice and water- or snow-covered roads are risky."],
 ["Climbing a coconut tree", "Legs push the trunk down; friction pushes the person up. Smooth trunks with less friction are harder to climb."],
 ["Canoe", "Paddle pushes water backward; water pushes paddle (and canoe) forward. A harder push gives a larger forward force and more speed (drag, currents, mass and rowing style also matter)."],
 ["Balloon–straw experiment / rocket", "Expelled air or gas is pushed out; the gas pushes the balloon or rocket in the opposite direction. Lift-off needs this upward force to exceed the rocket’s weight."],
 ["Rocket in space", "Firing the engine in the direction of motion slows it — used by Chandrayaan-3’s Vikram lander for a soft landing near the Moon’s south pole."],
 ["Firefighter with hose", "Water pushed forward exerts an equal backward force on the hose — hence the struggle."],
 ["Spacecraft with negligible gravity", "Changes its velocity by expelling gas (thrusters)."],
 ["Bullet and gun", "Gun recoils with force equal to that on the bullet"]]),
("table", ["Problem", "Working", "Result"], [
 ["Fruit and Earth pull each other equally; why does only the fruit move?", "a = F/m; Earth’s mass is enormous", "Earth’s acceleration is negligible"],
 ["Bullet 0.1 kg, gun 5 kg, force 2 N", "Gun: 2/5; bullet: 2/0.1", "0.4 m s<sup>−2</sup> and 20 m s<sup>−2</sup>"],
 ["Bar magnet and compass needle", "Equal forces; needle is much lighter", "Needle accelerates visibly, magnet does not"],
 ["Loaded cart hits identical empty cart", "Third law", "Equal forces on each"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "Equal forces ≠ equal accelerations: the accelerations differ when the masses differ. A sailor jumping forward from a boat pushes the boat backward (third law), so the boat moves backward."),

("h2", "6.7 Forces on a System of Objects"),
("ul", ["Two boxes (m<sub>1</sub>, m<sub>2</sub>) joined by a string on a frictionless surface, pulled by force F on box 1: the string’s **tension T** acts on box 1 to the left and on box 2 to the right (equal and opposite — third law).",
 "Treat both boxes and the string as one **system**: tension is an **internal force** and ignored; only **external forces** matter. Acceleration of the system: **a = F ÷ (m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>)**.",
 "External vertical forces — weights (m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>)g and normal forces — balance each other.",
 "Analysing box by box gives the same result. Science is often simpler when we study the whole instead of the parts (e.g. the overall motion of a walking person)."]),
("table", ["Law", "Statement", "Key idea", "Example"], [
 ["First", "No net force → no change in velocity", "Inertia", "Box pushed with force equal to friction moves uniformly"],
 ["Second", "F = ma", "Force causes acceleration", "Cricket catch, airbags"],
 ["Third", "Equal and opposite forces on different bodies", "Forces in pairs", "Rocket, rowing, walking"]]),
("note", "REMEMBER", "Newton’s laws work on scales from everyday objects to planets and stars; they need modification only near very massive objects, at speeds close to light, or at atomic scales."),

("qa", [
 ("State Newton’s first law. What is inertia?", "An object stays at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless a net force acts; inertia is the tendency to resist a change in state of rest or uniform motion."),
 ("State Newton’s second law and derive the unit of force.", "Acceleration is proportional to net force and inversely proportional to mass, F = ma; 1 N = 1 kg × 1 m s<sup>−2</sup>."),
 ("State Newton’s third law with two examples.", "Every force has an equal and opposite force on the other body; walking, rocket, rowing."),
 ("Why do action and reaction not cancel?", "They act on different objects."),
 ("Why does a fielder pull his hands back while catching a fast ball?", "Longer stopping time → smaller acceleration → smaller force and less injury."),
 ("Why are airbags provided in vehicles?", "They increase the time over which the passenger stops, reducing force."),
 ("How can friction be helpful? Give two uses.", "It enables walking, running and tyre grip; grooves on soles and tyre treads increase it."),
 ("Distinguish balanced and unbalanced forces.", "Balanced: equal, opposite, net force 0, no change in motion; unbalanced: net force ≠ 0, causing acceleration."),
 ("Calculate the weight of a 30 kg barbell.", "mg = 30 × 9.8 = 294 N."),
 ("Explain how a rocket lifts off.", "It expels gas downward; the gas pushes the rocket upward with an equal force, greater than its weight."),
 ("What did the coin-stack and spring-balance activities show?", "Friction depends on the nature of surfaces; smoother surface, smaller friction, longer travel."),
 ("Find the acceleration of two boxes of 4 kg and 6 kg pulled with 20 N on a smooth table.", "a = 20 ÷ (4 + 6) = 2 m s<sup>−2</sup>.")]),
],
takeaways=["Force is a vector (SI unit newton); only the net force decides motion — balanced forces leave motion unchanged.",
 "Friction opposes motion and depends on the surfaces; it is also what lets us walk and vehicles grip.",
 "First law: no net force → constant velocity (inertia). Second law: F = ma; weight = mg. Third law: forces come in equal, opposite pairs on different objects.",
 "Increasing the time of stopping reduces force (catching, airbags, mats).",
 "A connected system accelerates as a = F ÷ total mass; internal forces such as tension cancel out."])
